WHAT YOU ALREADY KNOW   What you already know
Chapter 7 computed the marginal revenue of a price cut: dropping the jar to $11.50 to sell 300,000 more raises revenue by $1,700,000 but costs more than it brings. Chapter 10 said that in a market-priced business the price is given. This chapter explains both, and measures the first.
11.1  Demand, supply and the price between them
A demand curve shows the quantity buyers will take at each price, and it slopes downward: lower prices bring more buyers and more purchases from each. A supply curve shows the quantity sellers will offer at each price, and it slopes upward: higher prices make production worthwhile for more sellers and for more output from each.
The price at which the two quantities are equal is the equilibrium price, and it is where a competitive market settles. Above it there are unsold goods and the price falls; below it there are unsatisfied buyers and the price rises.
The outcome asks what moves each curve, and the exam distinguishes two things carefully.
A movement along a curve is a response to a change in the price of the good itself. Nothing has shifted; the market is reading off a different point.
A shift of the whole curve is a response to a change in something else. Demand shifts with incomes, with the prices of substitutes and complements, with tastes, with the number of buyers and with expectations. Supply shifts with input costs, technology, the number of sellers, taxes and subsidies, and expectations.
For Orontes, the practical readings are immediate. A rise in the price of a competing spread shifts the demand for tahini to the right, because the two are substitutes. A rise in the price of bread, which is eaten with it, shifts demand to the left, because the two are complements. A fall in the sesame price shifts supply to the right, lowering the equilibrium price for everybody — which is why a cost saving in a competitive market is often competed away rather than kept.
Figure F711-02 sets the shifters out.
Figure F711-02. What moves demand and supply
@table
Curve | Moves right when | Moves left when
Demand | Incomes rise; a substitute's price rises; tastes favour the product; more buyers | Incomes fall; a complement's price rises; tastes move away
Supply | Input costs fall; technology improves; more sellers; a subsidy | Input costs rise; sellers leave; a tax is imposed
@end
