11.2  Four market structures
How much freedom a company has to set its own price depends on the structure of its market, and the outcome names four.
Pure competition. Many small sellers, an identical product, free entry and exit, and buyers who know the prices. No seller can charge more than the market price, because buyers would simply go elsewhere, and none needs to charge less, because each can sell all it wants at the market price. The company is a price taker: its demand curve is horizontal at the market price, so price equals marginal revenue, and in the short run it produces where marginal cost equals that price. Profits above the normal level attract entrants, which pushes the price down until only a normal return remains. Agricultural commodities are the usual example.
Monopolistic competition. Many sellers, but a differentiated product — by brand, recipe, packaging, service or location. Each seller has a little pricing power, because its product is not quite the same as the others, and its demand curve slopes gently downward. It can raise the price without losing every customer, and it loses some. Entry is free, so economic profits are competed away in the long run, usually by rivals imitating the differentiation. Branded food is the standard example, and it is where Orontes sits.
Oligopoly. Few sellers, each large enough that its actions affect the others. The defining feature is interdependence: a price change by one will be answered, so each seller must consider the response before moving. Prices are often sticky, because a cut will be matched — losing margin without gaining share — while a rise may not be followed, losing share without gaining margin. Competition shifts into advertising, quality and product variety, where responses are slower. Supermarkets, airlines and mobile operators are examples.
Monopoly. One seller of a product with no close substitutes, protected by a barrier — a patent, a licence, control of a resource, or an economy of scale so large that a second entrant could not survive. The monopolist faces the whole market demand curve and chooses the point on it that maximises profit, producing where marginal revenue equals marginal cost and charging what the curve will bear at that quantity. Because marginal revenue is below price, the monopoly price is above the competitive one and the quantity is lower, which is why monopolies are regulated.
Figure F711-03 compares the four.
Figure F711-03. Four structures, four degrees of pricing power
@table
Structure | Sellers | Product | Pricing power | Short-run price set where
Pure competition | Very many | Identical | None; a price taker | Marginal cost equals the market price
Monopolistic competition | Many | Differentiated | Some, limited by close substitutes | Marginal revenue equals marginal cost
Oligopoly | Few | Either | Considerable, limited by rivals' responses | Interdependently; prices often sticky
Monopoly | One | No close substitute | Greatest, limited by demand and regulation | Marginal revenue equals marginal cost
@end
The rule that unites the last three is Chapter 7's: produce while marginal revenue exceeds marginal cost, and stop where they are equal. Only in pure competition does that collapse into producing where marginal cost equals the price, because only there is marginal revenue equal to price.
