11.3  Cartels
A cartel is an agreement among sellers to act as if they were one. Members agree a price, or divide the market by quantity or territory, or both, and the effect is to convert competition into a shared monopoly: the price rises towards the monopoly price, the quantity falls, and the profits are divided among the members.
Three features of cartels are examinable.
They raise prices above the competitive level. That is their purpose, and it is why the arrangement is a criminal offence in most jurisdictions when practised by private companies. The exam's textbook example, the oil producers' organisation, is an agreement between states rather than companies and sits outside those laws.
They are unstable. Each member gains by cheating: with the price held up by the others, an individual seller who quietly shades its price or exceeds its quota sells more at a high price. Since every member faces that incentive, cartels require monitoring and punishment to survive, and most collapse — through cheating, through entry by outsiders attracted by the high price, or through a fall in demand that makes the agreed quotas unbearable.
They are detectable by their effects. Prices that move together without a common cost cause, bids that rotate among suppliers, identical quotations, and prices that are sticky upwards and never downwards are the classic signals, and a management accountant who sees them in a supplier market has a reporting obligation to consider.
Tacit collusion is the version without an agreement: firms in a concentrated market may arrive at similar prices simply by watching each other, with no communication at all. It is harder to prosecute and produces similar effects, which is one reason regulators scrutinise concentrated markets even where no agreement can be shown.
Figure F711-04 sets out the effects.
Figure F711-04. What a cartel does
@table
Effect | Direction | Who gains | Who loses
Price | Rises towards the monopoly price | The members | Buyers
Quantity | Falls | — | Buyers, and suppliers to the industry
Profits | Rise and are shared | The members | —
Stability | Falls over time | — | The members themselves
Entry | Attracted by high prices | Entrants | The members
@end
TERM BRIDGE   Key words in this section
@table
