11.4  Elasticity
Price elasticity of demand measures how much the quantity demanded responds to a change in price. It is the percentage change in quantity divided by the percentage change in price, and because the two move in opposite directions the result is negative — so it is conventionally quoted as a positive number and the exam does the same.
The midpoint formula avoids an awkward feature of the simple calculation: the answer differs depending on which end you start from. It solves this by dividing each change by the average of the two values rather than by the starting value.
WORKED EXAMPLE   Worked example: the elasticity of Orontes's demand
Step 1. The two points. At $12.00 the company sells 3,500,000 jars; at $11.50 it would sell 3,800,000.
Step 2. The percentage change in quantity. The change is 300,000 and the average quantity is 3,650,000, so the percentage change is 300,000 ÷ 3,650,000 = 8.22 per cent.
Step 3. The percentage change in price. The change is 0.50 and the average price is 11.75, so the percentage change is 0.50 ÷ 11.75 = 4.26 per cent.
Step 4. The elasticity. 8.22 ÷ 4.26 = 1.93.
Check. An elasticity above 1 means demand is elastic: quantity responds more than proportionately, so the price cut raises total revenue. And it does: 11.50 × 3,800,000 = 43,700,000 against $42,000,000. But Chapter 7 showed the same move lowers operating income, because contribution falls to 3,800,000 × 4.30 = 16,340,000 from $16,800,000 — a loss of 460,000. Elasticity tells you what happens to revenue. Only the contribution margin tells you what happens to profit, and the two can point in opposite directions, as they do here.
The vocabulary is fixed and the exam uses it precisely.
Elastic demand has an elasticity above 1. Quantity responds more than price, so a price cut raises total revenue and a price rise lowers it. Products with many substitutes, products that take a large share of a buyer's budget, and luxuries tend to be elastic, and demand is more elastic over a long period than a short one, because buyers have time to find alternatives.
Inelastic demand has an elasticity below 1. Quantity responds less than price, so a price rise raises total revenue. Necessities, products with no close substitutes, products that are a small part of a budget, and addictive goods tend to be inelastic.
Unitary elasticity is exactly 1, where total revenue does not change at all.
Figure F711-05 sets out the consequences.
Figure F711-05. Elasticity and total revenue
@table
Elasticity | Demand is | A price rise | A price cut | Typical products
Above 1 | Elastic | Lowers total revenue | Raises total revenue | Luxuries, many substitutes
Equal to 1 | Unitary | Leaves revenue unchanged | Leaves revenue unchanged | The boundary case
Below 1 | Inelastic | Raises total revenue | Lowers total revenue | Necessities, few substitutes
@end
EXAM TRAP   Revenue is not profit
An elastic demand means a price cut raises revenue. Whether it raises profit depends on the contribution margin, and Orontes is the standing example of a cut that does one and not the other.
Use the midpoint formula whenever the question says so, and say which formula you used when it does not. The simple formula gives a different answer in each direction.
Elasticity is not a property of a product for ever. It differs between the short run and the long run, between one price range and another on the same curve, and between markets.
