11.5  Estimating revenue from a price change
The last outcome asks for a revenue estimate given a price change and an elasticity, which is the calculation run in reverse.
The method is three steps. Multiply the percentage change in price by the elasticity to get the percentage change in quantity, in the opposite direction. Apply that to the current quantity. Multiply the new quantity by the new price.
WORKED EXAMPLE   Worked example: a price rise in an inelastic market
Step 1. The proposal. Orontes raises the jar by 5 per cent, from $12.00 to $12.60. Market research puts the elasticity of its branded demand at about 0.41 — inelastic, because loyal buyers have no exact substitute.
Step 2. The quantity response. Volume falls about 2 per cent, from 3,500,000 to 3,430,000 jars.
Step 3. The new revenue. 12.60 × 3,430,000 = 43,218,000 against $42,000,000, a rise of $1,218,000.
Step 4. The profit effect, which is the point. The contribution margin becomes 12.60 − 7.20 = 5.40, so contribution is 3,430,000 × 5.40 = 18,522,000 against $16,800,000 — a gain of 1,722,000, or 41 per cent of the company's operating income.
Check. Compare this with the price cut of section 11.4, which raised revenue by $1,700,000 and cut profit by $460,000. The two moves are almost mirror images in revenue and opposite in profit, and the reason is the contribution margin: at 40 per cent, Orontes loses $4.80 on every jar it does not sell and gains only the price change on every jar it does. A company with a high contribution margin should be slow to cut price and quick to consider raising it, which is the arithmetic behind the warning Book 1 issued and Chapter 2 ranked as the most powerful of the four levers.
Figure F711-06 sets the two scenarios out.
Figure F711-06. The same company, two price moves
@table
| Price cut to 11.50 | Price rise to 12.60
Elasticity assumed | 1.93 | 0.41
Volume | 3,800,000 | 3,430,000
Total revenue | 43,700,000 | 43,218,000
Contribution margin a jar | 4.30 | 5.40
Total contribution | 16,340,000 | 18,522,000
Change in operating income | (460,000) | 1,722,000
@end
Three cautions close the chapter, and an exam answer should carry at least one.
An elasticity is an estimate, usually from a narrow range of prices and a particular period. Applying it to a large price change extrapolates beyond the evidence.
It holds other things equal, and competitors are not other things. In the oligopoly of section 11.2, a price rise that rivals do not follow has a far higher effective elasticity than one they match.
And the long run differs from the short. A price rise that loses 2 per cent of volume this year may lose far more over three years as buyers find alternatives and rivals position against it — which is the mirror of Book 1's point that a price concession is permanent in a way a volume gain is not.
