@end
LANGUAGE FOCUS   "Elastic"
English elastic in economics means responsive, and the metaphor is of something that stretches a long way when pulled. The everyday sense of flexible or accommodating is close enough to be misleading: an elastic demand is not a forgiving one, it is a demand that punishes a price rise severely. Arabic مرن carries the same everyday pull towards flexibility and must be read technically. French élastique is the technical term and souple the everyday one, so the French reader is less likely to blur them. Note the companion trap in all three languages: inelastic does not mean rigid in the sense of unchanging — an inelastic demand still falls when the price rises, just less than proportionately.
