Chapter summary
Figure F711-08 brings the chapter together.
Figure F711-08. Chapter 11 summary map: the key rule for each section.
Demand slopes down, supply slopes up, and the price between them clears the market; a change in the good's own price moves along a curve while a change in anything else shifts it, so a substitute's price rise lifts demand and an industry-wide cost fall lowers the equilibrium price for everybody. Four structures give four degrees of freedom: pure competition makes a price taker whose marginal revenue equals price, monopolistic competition gives a little power through differentiation, oligopoly makes every move interdependent and prices sticky, and monopoly lets one seller choose a point on the whole demand curve — with all but the first producing where marginal revenue equals marginal cost. A cartel converts competition into shared monopoly, raising price and cutting quantity, and it is unstable because each member gains by cheating. Elasticity measures the response: for Orontes, 300,000 ÷ 3,650,000 = 8.22 per cent of quantity against 0.50 ÷ 11.75 = 4.26 per cent of price gives 8.22 ÷ 4.26 = 1.93, which is elastic, so the cut raises revenue to 11.50 × 3,800,000 = 43,700,000 and still lowers operating income by 460,000. And run the other way, a 5 per cent rise against an elasticity of 0.41 takes contribution to 3,430,000 × 5.40 = 18,522,000, a gain of 1,722,000 — which is why a company with a 40 per cent contribution margin should be slow to cut and quick to consider raising.
