Case-style practice: the premium jar meets the market
Orontes's premium 500 gram jar, priced at $9.25 in Chapter 10, faces two competing brands at $9.50. Marketing estimates that at $8.75 the company would sell 780,000 jars instead of 600,000, and that at $9.75 it would sell 480,000. The variable cost is $5.60 a jar and the line's fixed costs are $900,000.
Required. Compute the elasticity between $8.75 and $9.75 using the midpoint formula, the revenue and the operating income at each of the three prices, and recommend. Under 300 words.
Answers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.
Answers and explanations
Section checks
SC11-1  Answer A. Only the good's own price moves along the curve.
B is wrong. Shifts come from other variables.
C is wrong. The supply curve responds to its own shifters.
D is wrong. Quantity demanded changes.
SC11-2  Answer A. Buyers move towards the cheaper alternative, which is this product.
B is wrong. That is the effect of a complement's price rise.
C is wrong. Substitutes are a classic shifter.
D is wrong. Curves shift horizontally in this analysis.
SC11-3  Answer A. More is supplied at every price, so the price falls.
B is wrong. Demand is unaffected by sellers' costs.
C is wrong. Greater supply lowers the price.
D is wrong. In a competitive market the saving is largely competed away.
SC11-4  Answer A. A price taker sells all it wants at the market price.
B is wrong. No seller has that freedom there.
C is wrong. The individual seller's curve is horizontal.
D is wrong. Every producer has a marginal cost.
SC11-5  Answer A. The asymmetry of responses deters movement.
B is wrong. Oligopolies exist with differentiated products too.
C is wrong. Entry is usually restricted.
D is wrong. Perfect elasticity belongs to pure competition.
SC11-6  Answer A. The general rule for any price setter.
B is wrong. That is the competitive outcome.
C is wrong. Minimum average cost is not the profit-maximising rule.
D is wrong. A monopolist faces a downward-sloping curve.
SC11-7  Answer A. Price up, quantity down.
B is wrong. The price rises.
C is wrong. The quantity falls.
D is wrong. Both change, which is the purpose.
SC11-8  Answer A. Cheating is individually profitable.
B is wrong. Cartel prices rise.
C is wrong. Demand is not always rising.
D is wrong. Detection is difficult, which is why monitoring matters.
SC11-9  Answer A. Tacit collusion needs no agreement.
B is wrong. A cartel involves an agreement.
C is wrong. Pure competition has no such coordination.
D is wrong. Price discrimination is charging different buyers differently.
SC11-10  Answer A. Each change is divided by the average of the two values.
B is wrong. That is the simple formula.
C is wrong. The ending value gives a different answer again.
D is wrong. There is no such convention.
SC11-11  Answer A. Above 1 is elastic.
B is wrong. Below 1 is inelastic.
C is wrong. Unitary is exactly 1.
D is wrong. Perfect inelasticity is zero.
SC11-12  Answer A. Revenue and profit can move in opposite directions.
B is wrong. Orontes is the counter-example.
C is wrong. Rising revenue on a cut proves elasticity.
D is wrong. The contribution per unit falls with the price.
SC11-13  Answer A. Elasticity converts a price change into a quantity change.
B is wrong. The margin converts quantity into profit.
C is wrong. The markup is a pricing device.
D is wrong. Market share is an outcome.
SC11-14  Answer A. Extrapolation goes beyond the evidence.
B is wrong. Elasticity varies along the curve.
C is wrong. It varies with time and market.
D is wrong. Competitors' responses change it materially.
Your turn
@table
Item | Answer
Percentage change in quantity | 9.52 per cent: the change is 4,000 and the average quantity is 42,000, so 4,000 ÷ 42,000 = 9.52 per cent.
Percentage change in price | 5.13 per cent: the change is 1.00 and the average price is 19.50, so 1.00 ÷ 19.50 = 5.13 per cent.
Elasticity, midpoint | 1.86: 9.52 ÷ 5.13 = 1.86.
Elastic or inelastic? | Elastic, because the figure exceeds 1.
Effect on total revenue | Revenue rises: 19.00 × 44,000 = 836,000 against 20.00 × 40,000 = 800,000.
New revenue after a 4 per cent price rise at an elasticity of 0.60 | 1,015,040: quantity falls 2.4 per cent to 48,800 and the price rises to 20.80, so 20.80 × 48,800 = 1,015,040 against 1,000,000 before.
What it tells you | The same product is elastic in one range and assumed inelastic in another, which is the ordinary state of affairs: elasticity is a local measurement, not a label.
@end
Multiple-choice questions
P11-01  Answer A. Income is a shifter.
B is wrong. Only the good's own price moves along the curve.
C is wrong. Supply responds to costs and sellers.
D is wrong. Demand moves with income.
P11-02  Answer A. A price taker's own curve is horizontal.
B is wrong. That is the market curve, and the curve facing a price setter.
C is wrong. A vertical curve is perfectly inelastic.
D is wrong. Demand curves do not slope upward.
P11-03  Answer A. Many sellers, differentiated products.
B is wrong. That is monopoly.
C is wrong. That is closer to oligopoly.
D is wrong. Entry is free in monopolistic competition.
P11-04  Answer A. The asymmetry of responses.
B is wrong. Oligopoly does not require identical products.
C is wrong. Perfect elasticity is a competitive feature.
D is wrong. Entry is usually limited.
P11-05  Answer A. Towards the monopoly price.
B is wrong. Lowering the price defeats the purpose.
C is wrong. Output falls.
D is wrong. High prices attract entry, which members fear.
P11-06  Answer A. 4,000 ÷ 42,000 = 9.52 per cent.
B is wrong. That is the simple formula on the starting value.
C is wrong. That uses the ending value.
D is wrong. This has no basis in the figures.
P11-07  Answer A. 1.00 ÷ 19.50 = 5.13 per cent.
B is wrong. That uses the starting price.
C is wrong. That uses the ending price.
D is wrong. That is the change in dollars.
P11-08  Answer A. 9.52 ÷ 5.13 = 1.86.
B is wrong. This inverts the ratio.
C is wrong. This uses rounded simple percentages.
D is wrong. This inverts a rounded figure.
P11-09  Answer A. Inelastic demand rewards a price rise in revenue.
B is wrong. Elastic is above 1.
C is wrong. Unitary is exactly 1.
D is wrong. Perfect elasticity is infinite.
P11-10  Answer A. Contribution can fall while revenue rises.
B is wrong. Orontes is the counter-example.
C is wrong. Revenue rose, by assumption.
D is wrong. It proves elasticity, not inelasticity.
Case-style set: the premium jar meets the market
The elasticity. Between $8.75 and $9.75 the quantity falls from 780,000 to 480,000, a change of 300,000 on an average of 630,000, which is 300,000 ÷ 630,000 = 47.62 per cent. The price changes by 1.00 on an average of 9.25, which is 1.00 ÷ 9.25 = 10.81 per cent. The elasticity is 47.62 ÷ 10.81 = 4.41 — highly elastic, as a premium product facing two close substitutes at $9.50 should be.
The three outcomes. At $8.75 the contribution is 8.75 − 5.60 = 3.15 a jar, so 780,000 × 3.15 = 2,457,000 of contribution and 2,457,000 − 900,000 = 1,557,000 of operating income, on revenue of 8.75 × 780,000 = 6,825,000. At $9.25, the contribution is 3.65 and 600,000 × 3.65 = 2,190,000 gives 1,290,000 of operating income on revenue of $5,550,000. At $9.75 the contribution is 4.15 and 480,000 × 4.15 = 1,992,000 gives 1,092,000, on revenue of $4,680,000.
Recommendation: price at $8.75. It earns the most operating income of the three by $267,000, and it is the only one of the three that is clearly below both competitors rather than beside them. The elasticity of 4.41 is what makes the low price pay: in this range the company gains far more volume than it gives up in margin, which is the opposite of the standard jar's position in Chapter 11's main example.
Two cautions. The volume figures are marketing estimates across a wide price range, and the break-even volume at $8.75 is 900,000 ÷ 3.15 = 285,714 jars, so there is room for the estimate to be wrong. And at $8.75 the premium jar may cannibalise the standard line, which contributes $4.80 — a loss of margin on every switched buyer that this analysis has not counted.
